## What is wrong

A `@nothrow` function that narrows a union and then reads a getter is reported clean, even when the getter that really runs throws. This program reports nothing today; drop the `instanceof` guard and it is correctly reported as `inferredThrowingHiddenTransfer`:

```ts
class Safe {
  get p(): number { return 1; }
}
class Risky {
  get p(): number { throw new Error("boom"); }
}

/** @nothrow */
export function reads(): number {
  let o: Safe | Risky = new Safe();
  const swap = (): void => { o = new Risky(); };
  if (!(o instanceof Safe)) return 0;
  swap();
  return o.p;                 // runs Risky's getter, reported clean
}
```

A narrowing is a fact about one path, and TypeScript keeps one across an assignment it did not follow: a closure writing the binding, or — for a property — any call at all. #137 fixed exactly this where a condition is discharged, by reading the declaration's type instead of the checker's type at the reference. Every read that answers what a *hidden* transfer enters still had the fault, and the same shape reaches the consumption and promise paths.

## What changed

`assignableType` from #137 becomes `settledType`, exported and shared by the three files that ask what a value can be. It gains what this fault needs beyond #137's scope: a member access, settled recursively so a path is read off the arms its root was declared with rather than the arm a guard picked; a destructured `let`; and an imported `export let`, which is immutable at the import and writable in the module that declared it.

Settling the receiver alone did not fix the repro. A named property resolved its member through `checker.getSymbolAtLocation`, which hands back the member of the arm the narrowing picked — the same fault one level along — so the lookup now runs on the settled receiver and joins the arms the declaration admits. The same read now answers a keyed access, a destructuring (whose pattern takes its type from the initializer *as narrowed*, so the source is walked back to what the syntax names, nested patterns included), a coercion (whose narrowing to a primitive removed the site altogether), an `instanceof`, a consumption's join over constituents, and the test that decides whether a value can reject at all.

Two type reads keep the narrowing on purpose, and the reasons are worth a look during review:

- `awaitedDirectly` asks after a call expression. That is not a reference, so no narrowing exists there to read.
- `returnedIterators` and the `it.next()` gate ask whether there is a protocol question here at all. A narrowing can only ever *add* a question at a gate, and settling these would lose reports rather than gain them, because `isIteratorType` asks `getPropertyOfType`, which answers nothing for a mixed union such as `Iterator<T> | number`. What those gates hand on is answered by `consumedBy`, which now settles. The mixed union is silent with or without a narrowing, so that residue is union-blindness rather than this fault.

## What it costs

A guard on a writable binding whose declared union carries a throwing accessor on the arm the guard excluded is now reported: `function f(o: Safe | Risky) { if (o instanceof Safe) return o.p; }`. Binding to a `const` first is the way back to green, since nothing can write one, and every new fixture pins that with a control.

Three things that look like they would be taxed and are not: narrowing away `null` or `undefined` (the other arms carry no member to join), discriminating a union of data properties (a data property runs nothing), and calling a method (a call resolves through the checker's resolved signature, which this does not touch).

That last one is an asymmetry rather than a mercy. `o.m()` on a `Safe | Risky` reports nothing today **even with no narrowing anywhere**, because `getResolvedSignature` picks one arm — the same union-blindness as `isIteratorType`. It is out of scope here and neither is reachable from this fix; both want a ticket of their own.

## How it was checked

Every site listed above was first shown to be a hole by a program that reported nothing and should have, and then shown to report after the change — the repro, a mutable-property receiver, a keyed read, a plain and a nested destructuring, a destructuring assignment, a coercion, an `instanceof`, a `for…of`, an `await`, and a returned promise.

Five fixtures pin the behaviour, each with a `const` control that must stay clean. `pnpm test` is green: 243 fixtures under both the hybrid and the declare-only pass, 17 CLI cases, plus the lockstep, docs and diagnostics audits. The 238 pre-existing fixtures needed no edits, so nothing this suite already asserted relied on reading a narrowing at these sites.

